1
00:00:02,018 --> 00:00:03,938
[NARRATOR]
The end of the 19th century.

2
00:00:03,979 --> 00:00:07,608
Amidst the upheaval caused
by the industrial revolution,

3
00:00:07,650 --> 00:00:10,653
Britain steadily
expands its colonization.

4
00:00:10,694 --> 00:00:13,781
Eventually its territories cover
one quarter of the earth--

5
00:00:13,823 --> 00:00:17,242
an empire on which
the sun never sets.

6
00:00:17,284 --> 00:00:20,412
Despite being the
greatest dominion in history,

7
00:00:20,454 --> 00:00:22,539
the land is ruled
by the affluent,

8
00:00:22,581 --> 00:00:26,543
which makes up less than three
percent of the total population.

9
00:00:26,585 --> 00:00:30,339
In steep contrast with rapid
advancement of technology,

10
00:00:30,380 --> 00:00:34,551
an archaic class structure
remains firmly rooted in place.

11
00:00:34,593 --> 00:00:38,180
This system rigidly divides
people into social categories

12
00:00:38,221 --> 00:00:41,725
where an individual's merit
is determined by the family

13
00:00:41,767 --> 00:00:44,311
they're born into,
and as a result,

14
00:00:44,353 --> 00:00:48,064
inevitably creates
widespread discrimination.

15
00:01:04,164 --> 00:01:07,167
[WILLIAM] I take it
you're a gambling man?

16
00:01:08,794 --> 00:01:10,086
Forgive the intrusion.

17
00:01:10,128 --> 00:01:13,298
I couldn't help but notice
you studying the racing sheet.

18
00:01:13,340 --> 00:01:15,091
Me? A gambler?

19
00:01:15,133 --> 00:01:18,261
I'm a bit more of
a dabbler, really. My wife,

20
00:01:18,303 --> 00:01:21,223
well, she's always telling me
if I have enough for the horses,

21
00:01:21,264 --> 00:01:24,393
I should replace
the wallpaper first.

22
00:01:24,434 --> 00:01:28,939
I see. Then perhaps I could
be of some assistance to you.

23
00:01:28,981 --> 00:01:30,858
I may be able to
afford you the means

24
00:01:30,900 --> 00:01:32,568
to acquire that new wallpaper

25
00:01:32,609 --> 00:01:35,153
as well as a bouquet
of flowers for your wife.

26
00:01:35,195 --> 00:01:36,572
If I didn't know better,

27
00:01:36,613 --> 00:01:40,075
it sounds like you know
which horses will win.

28
00:01:40,116 --> 00:01:42,912
Once you analyze the data,
you can predict the outcome.

29
00:01:42,953 --> 00:01:45,497
It's exactly the same
as any other game.

30
00:01:45,539 --> 00:01:47,041
[WILLIAM chuckles]

31
00:03:23,553 --> 00:03:26,682
[LABOURER 2A] So then, what
about the upcoming Wessex Cup?

32
00:03:26,723 --> 00:03:28,308
Which horse to bet on.

33
00:03:28,350 --> 00:03:30,435
Might I suggest
the one who'll win it?

34
00:03:30,477 --> 00:03:33,522
Right, like anybody could
know that ahead of time.

35
00:03:33,563 --> 00:03:34,815
[WILLIAM]
John Striker's steed.

36
00:03:34,857 --> 00:03:36,525
Huh?

37
00:03:37,484 --> 00:03:38,902
[WILLIAM]
I said the horse belonging to

38
00:03:38,944 --> 00:03:42,489
the trainer, John Striker,
will win the race.

39
00:03:45,951 --> 00:03:47,786
Oh, yeah? And you're certain?

40
00:03:47,828 --> 00:03:50,998
Nothing in life is certain,
but the odds are favourable.

41
00:03:51,040 --> 00:03:52,457
You know,

42
00:03:52,499 --> 00:03:55,127
somehow this kid also predicted
the winners of the last race.

43
00:03:55,169 --> 00:03:58,338
Tell me, lad, you able
to see into the future?

44
00:03:58,380 --> 00:04:02,009
No, sir, but horse racing
is like any other game.

45
00:04:02,051 --> 00:04:06,596
By studying the figures, you
can win 80 percent of the time.

46
00:04:06,638 --> 00:04:08,182
[WILLIAM chuckles]

47
00:04:08,223 --> 00:04:10,809
[GROUP grunts]

48
00:04:12,268 --> 00:04:14,563
[LABOURER 2B]
"Studying the figures," he says.

49
00:04:14,604 --> 00:04:17,399
Can you really predict
winners by tallying numbers?

50
00:04:17,440 --> 00:04:18,734
[LABOURER 2C]
Search me.

51
00:04:18,775 --> 00:04:21,070
Well... he is upper
crust, after all.

52
00:04:21,111 --> 00:04:25,199
Boy's got that fancy learning,
not like us commoners.

53
00:04:25,240 --> 00:04:26,241
[ROADSIDE MERCHANT 2A]
Young master!

54
00:04:26,282 --> 00:04:27,117
Uh?

55
00:04:27,159 --> 00:04:29,912
I'm sorry,
but I need your advice.

56
00:04:29,953 --> 00:04:31,580
It's the flowers I sell.

57
00:04:31,621 --> 00:04:36,001
When I keep them at home,
they wither up.

58
00:04:36,043 --> 00:04:38,461
I would suggest adding
just a splash of vinegar

59
00:04:38,503 --> 00:04:40,463
to whichever pail
you use to water them.

60
00:04:40,505 --> 00:04:42,132
Oh! Some vinegar?

61
00:04:42,174 --> 00:04:44,259
And that'll keep
them looking fresh?

62
00:04:44,300 --> 00:04:47,096
Yes... but only a little bit.

63
00:04:47,137 --> 00:04:48,513
[BAKER 2A]
There he is!

64
00:04:48,555 --> 00:04:50,766
I wanted to thank you
for all your help!

65
00:04:50,807 --> 00:04:52,976
I set out some strychnine,
just like you said,

66
00:04:53,018 --> 00:04:56,146
and the rats, poof,
completely disappeared.

67
00:04:56,188 --> 00:04:58,565
For you. A token of my thanks.

68
00:04:58,607 --> 00:05:02,527
You're too kind.
I gladly accept.

69
00:05:10,660 --> 00:05:12,328
[WILLIAM]
Brother.

70
00:05:12,370 --> 00:05:14,206
Out and about town, I see.

71
00:05:14,248 --> 00:05:17,167
Indeed. I'd business
to attend to.

72
00:05:17,209 --> 00:05:19,753
But headed home.
Do you care to join me?

73
00:05:19,795 --> 00:05:20,837
[WILLIAM]
Sure.

74
00:05:20,879 --> 00:05:22,338
A smartly dressed lad like that,

75
00:05:22,380 --> 00:05:24,382
you'd think I'd
know who they were.

76
00:05:24,424 --> 00:05:27,010
Those are the sons
of the Moriarty family.

77
00:05:27,052 --> 00:05:28,262
[OLD WOMAN 2A grunts]

78
00:05:28,303 --> 00:05:30,388
[BAKER 2A] On the left is
the oldest, Mister Albert,

79
00:05:30,430 --> 00:05:33,267
and the other is Master William.

80
00:05:33,308 --> 00:05:36,186
It's strange.
That younger brother,

81
00:05:36,228 --> 00:05:40,107
I could've sworn he looked
different than that before.

82
00:05:47,489 --> 00:05:50,659
[ALBERT] You seem to
be as popular as ever.

83
00:05:50,700 --> 00:05:53,745
So what sort of advice
were they in need of this time?

84
00:05:53,787 --> 00:05:55,205
Just a few simple matters

85
00:05:55,247 --> 00:05:57,624
pertaining to
horticulture and gambling.

86
00:05:57,666 --> 00:06:00,543
You've become quite the
consultant to the townspeople

87
00:06:00,585 --> 00:06:03,213
regardless of their predicament.

88
00:06:03,255 --> 00:06:05,257
Continue building
that mind palace

89
00:06:05,299 --> 00:06:08,010
until it becomes
a wonder of the world.

90
00:06:08,051 --> 00:06:10,804
And a benefit to all the people.

91
00:06:10,846 --> 00:06:14,057
Thank you.
I am endeavouring to do so.

92
00:06:15,725 --> 00:06:17,644
[WILLIAM]
I nearly forgot.

93
00:06:17,686 --> 00:06:20,105
A gift from the baker whom
I advised the other day

94
00:06:20,147 --> 00:06:22,316
on how to deal with
his mouse problems.

95
00:06:22,357 --> 00:06:23,317
[ALBERT]
Then I suppose you could

96
00:06:23,358 --> 00:06:25,652
call them your "just desserts."

97
00:06:25,694 --> 00:06:27,570
You really ought to
share them with Louis.

98
00:06:27,612 --> 00:06:30,406
I won't breathe
a word of it to anyone.

99
00:06:30,448 --> 00:06:32,701
That's very kind of you.

100
00:06:32,742 --> 00:06:39,249
[ALBERT] No. If my parents find
out, there'll be hell to pay.

101
00:06:39,291 --> 00:06:41,459
It seems like we're
partners in crime.

102
00:06:41,501 --> 00:06:43,253
[WILLIAM chuckles]

103
00:06:49,551 --> 00:06:53,138
Uh! Louis!

104
00:06:53,180 --> 00:06:55,515
Welcome back home, brother.

105
00:06:55,557 --> 00:06:57,100
[coughs]

106
00:06:57,142 --> 00:06:57,809
[WILLIAM]
What are you doing?

107
00:06:57,851 --> 00:07:01,021
You should still be in bed.

108
00:07:01,063 --> 00:07:02,647
[SIMON]
The boy's limbs work.

109
00:07:02,689 --> 00:07:06,109
So, I don't see why
he can't be of use.

110
00:07:06,151 --> 00:07:07,736
But never mind him.

111
00:07:07,777 --> 00:07:11,240
Who gave you permission to
ride in the family carriage?

112
00:07:11,281 --> 00:07:13,242
[ALBERT]
I did.

113
00:07:13,283 --> 00:07:14,951
[SIMON]
Mister Albert!

114
00:07:14,993 --> 00:07:15,869
Welcome back home, sir--

115
00:07:15,911 --> 00:07:17,829
I let him ride along
in the carriage.

116
00:07:17,871 --> 00:07:20,916
Are you satisfied?

117
00:07:20,957 --> 00:07:24,253
There's no denying you have
a generous heart, Mister Albert.

118
00:07:24,294 --> 00:07:25,379
But you need to maintain

119
00:07:25,420 --> 00:07:27,463
an appropriate distance
from these two,

120
00:07:27,505 --> 00:07:29,799
or I'll be reprimanded
by her ladyship.

121
00:07:29,841 --> 00:07:32,677
Louis, Hide these.

122
00:07:32,719 --> 00:07:33,636
[LOUIS]
Right.

123
00:07:33,678 --> 00:07:36,556
Remove that coat immediately.

124
00:07:38,683 --> 00:07:41,353
You may be cloaked in
Mister Albert's hand-me-downs,

125
00:07:41,395 --> 00:07:44,814
but they're still wasted
on the likes of you.

126
00:07:46,566 --> 00:07:49,444
[SIMON] You dare dress
yourself in such costly attire

127
00:07:49,485 --> 00:07:52,114
and stroll around town?

128
00:07:52,155 --> 00:07:54,490
One could scarcely
believe not so long ago,

129
00:07:54,532 --> 00:07:58,578
you were living on the streets,
begging for scraps.

130
00:07:58,620 --> 00:08:02,999
To think, now I'm serving two
brats pulled from the gutter,

131
00:08:03,041 --> 00:08:04,584
lower than myself.

132
00:08:04,626 --> 00:08:06,086
There's no greater shame.

133
00:08:06,128 --> 00:08:09,631
[ALBERT]
Simon! You speak too freely.

134
00:08:09,672 --> 00:08:12,050
[SIMON] But, Mister Albert,
these waifs are--

135
00:08:12,092 --> 00:08:17,264
[WILLIAM]
What's all the commotion?

136
00:08:17,306 --> 00:08:20,058
Accept my apologies.

137
00:08:24,062 --> 00:08:26,522
[SIMON]
Master William.

138
00:08:43,457 --> 00:08:47,502
Going out? Where to?

139
00:08:47,543 --> 00:08:50,713
The school. You are aware
that's where I volunteer.

140
00:08:50,755 --> 00:08:52,882
Even if you are
doing it for charity,

141
00:08:52,924 --> 00:08:56,386
I still think you've got one
morbid sense of curiosity.

142
00:08:56,428 --> 00:08:59,014
It's beyond me why you would
choose to surround yourself

143
00:08:59,055 --> 00:09:01,516
with the disgusting
lower classes.

144
00:09:03,477 --> 00:09:05,061
[WILLIAM] It goes against
the very principles

145
00:09:05,103 --> 00:09:08,064
of being a noble. We do
everything we can to avoid them.

146
00:09:08,106 --> 00:09:11,026
Yet you run to
meet their embrace.

147
00:09:11,067 --> 00:09:13,362
Remember that cold
you had last week?

148
00:09:13,403 --> 00:09:16,156
I bet I know where
you caught it from.

149
00:09:24,247 --> 00:09:26,708
[ALBERT]
I hate it. Everything.

150
00:09:26,749 --> 00:09:29,669
This whole world.

151
00:09:29,711 --> 00:09:31,546
Our nation, and its
cursed caste system.

152
00:09:31,587 --> 00:09:35,384
A mark placed upon
the soul at birth.

153
00:09:35,425 --> 00:09:37,386
It's sickening.

154
00:09:41,306 --> 00:09:44,100
[ALBERT] I beg you,
forgive my absence last week,

155
00:09:44,142 --> 00:09:46,769
but it seems I caught
a bit of a cold.

156
00:09:46,811 --> 00:09:49,147
[TEACHER 2A] There's nothing
to forgive, Lord Moriarty.

157
00:09:49,189 --> 00:09:50,690
Your visits are the highpoint

158
00:09:50,732 --> 00:09:53,068
for the children and
brighten their day.

159
00:09:53,109 --> 00:09:55,737
But frankly, shouldn't
you be back at home

160
00:09:55,778 --> 00:09:59,741
resting and building
up your strength?

161
00:09:59,782 --> 00:10:04,329
I would, but it hardly
feels like home these days.

162
00:10:04,371 --> 00:10:05,621
Oh?

163
00:10:05,663 --> 00:10:07,457
[GIRL 2A] There you are!
Can you read this book to us?

164
00:10:07,499 --> 00:10:12,628
Of course. I'll be right there--

165
00:10:12,670 --> 00:10:13,963
[GIRL 2A giggles]

166
00:10:14,005 --> 00:10:18,009
Here! This is the book Mister
Moriarty gave us to read.

167
00:10:18,051 --> 00:10:19,511
[ALBERT]
Those two boys.

168
00:10:19,553 --> 00:10:21,388
I don't think
I've seen them before.

169
00:10:21,430 --> 00:10:22,764
Ah, yes.

170
00:10:22,805 --> 00:10:25,559
We took the brothers in just
last week while you were away.

171
00:10:25,600 --> 00:10:29,062
The younger one is so sickly
and frail, the poor thing.

172
00:10:29,104 --> 00:10:32,566
That aside, both are literate
and even know Latin.

173
00:10:32,607 --> 00:10:34,650
Moreover, the older boy
is knowledgeable

174
00:10:34,692 --> 00:10:36,236
in a wide range of subjects,

175
00:10:36,278 --> 00:10:38,654
specifically maths
and chemistry.

176
00:10:38,696 --> 00:10:42,325
Whenever he opens his mouth,
he leaves adults speechless.

177
00:10:42,367 --> 00:10:45,287
Before their arrival here, I
understand they would venture to

178
00:10:45,328 --> 00:10:48,498
the British Museum's
reading room unattended.

179
00:10:48,540 --> 00:10:50,792
Oddly enough,
the head librarian asked

180
00:10:50,833 --> 00:10:52,877
if we could take charge of them.

181
00:10:52,919 --> 00:10:54,129
[ALBERT]
An orphan

182
00:10:54,170 --> 00:10:57,840
with the intellect to leave
even adults speechless.

183
00:10:57,882 --> 00:11:01,928
Initially, I simply regarded him
as some sort of prodigy.

184
00:11:01,970 --> 00:11:03,638
That was all.

185
00:11:03,679 --> 00:11:06,308
Nothing else
triggered my interest.

186
00:11:06,349 --> 00:11:09,936
But that soon changed.

187
00:11:17,193 --> 00:11:20,738
Are you seriously
fostering an orphan?

188
00:11:20,780 --> 00:11:24,200
Yes! Don't you think
it's simply wonderful?

189
00:11:24,242 --> 00:11:26,911
By doing so,
we're saving some poor child

190
00:11:26,953 --> 00:11:30,165
from a fate they had
absolutely no control over.

191
00:11:30,206 --> 00:11:33,042
How kind of you.

192
00:11:33,084 --> 00:11:35,795
[VISCOUNT 2A clears throat]
I'm deeply moved, Your Grace.

193
00:11:35,837 --> 00:11:37,797
Not only by your
humanitarian views,

194
00:11:37,839 --> 00:11:40,342
but by the fact that you
put action behind them.

195
00:11:40,383 --> 00:11:43,136
Then you'll--

196
00:11:43,178 --> 00:11:44,429
Arrangements will be made

197
00:11:44,471 --> 00:11:46,097
to welcome an orphan
into my home

198
00:11:46,139 --> 00:11:48,057
and raise them like
they were my own.

199
00:11:48,099 --> 00:11:52,979
Sir! I just knew you had
a kind and charitable heart.

200
00:11:53,021 --> 00:11:53,896
[EARL MORIARTY grumbles]

201
00:11:53,938 --> 00:11:55,565
[MADAME PÉNIÈRES]
Lord Moriarty.

202
00:11:55,607 --> 00:12:00,069
May I count on you to
open up your heart as well?

203
00:12:00,111 --> 00:12:02,489
Uh, of course.

204
00:12:07,619 --> 00:12:08,995
[COUNTESS MORIARTY]
Are you mad?

205
00:12:09,037 --> 00:12:11,831
What do you mean,
we're taking in an orphan?

206
00:12:11,873 --> 00:12:14,959
My word is final.
There's no point in us arguing.

207
00:12:15,001 --> 00:12:16,545
And my word?

208
00:12:16,586 --> 00:12:19,255
Our family already has
two outstanding sons.

209
00:12:19,297 --> 00:12:20,674
Is that not good enough for you?

210
00:12:20,715 --> 00:12:21,799
What's worse,

211
00:12:21,841 --> 00:12:25,303
it'll be an orphan from
God only knows where!

212
00:12:25,803 --> 00:12:28,557
The nobility have
certain obligations,

213
00:12:28,598 --> 00:12:31,643
including acts of charity.
You should know this!

214
00:12:31,685 --> 00:12:35,397
Huh? It's the Duchess
Pénières, isn't it?

215
00:12:35,438 --> 00:12:37,732
The woman's obsessed
with helping the poor.

216
00:12:37,773 --> 00:12:40,443
She put you up
to this! Didn't she?!

217
00:12:40,485 --> 00:12:43,029
Give her this message:
we decline!

218
00:12:43,071 --> 00:12:44,989
Either you tell her, or I will!

219
00:12:45,031 --> 00:12:47,158
[EARL MORIARTY]
Don't be a fool? I couldn't!

220
00:12:47,200 --> 00:12:49,494
The shame of going
back on my word.

221
00:12:49,536 --> 00:12:51,329
[COUNTESS MORIARTY]
You must!

222
00:12:53,498 --> 00:12:55,709
[ALBERT]
They bloomed magnificently.

223
00:12:55,750 --> 00:12:57,085
Oh, yes.

224
00:12:57,126 --> 00:13:00,880
But only because I took that
young man's gardening advice.

225
00:13:00,922 --> 00:13:03,758
I'm not sure if he read it
in a book somewhere,

226
00:13:03,799 --> 00:13:07,429
but regardless, the range of
subjects he knows is astounding.

227
00:13:07,470 --> 00:13:10,223
So do you think that you could
teach us how to do maths?

228
00:13:10,265 --> 00:13:11,558
If you know your maths,

229
00:13:11,600 --> 00:13:13,685
you can make loads of money
in the city with a real job.

230
00:13:13,727 --> 00:13:15,811
No way! He was supposed to read

231
00:13:15,853 --> 00:13:17,772
from the next
chapter of our book!

232
00:13:17,813 --> 00:13:19,733
[BOY 2B] Never mind all
that school rubbish!

233
00:13:19,774 --> 00:13:21,234
Tell us how to win at chess!

234
00:13:21,276 --> 00:13:23,027
Children, really.

235
00:13:23,069 --> 00:13:25,863
You can't expect him to
fulfill all of your requests.

236
00:13:25,905 --> 00:13:28,324
But what about you, Teacher?
You asked him to make

237
00:13:28,366 --> 00:13:31,244
some cough medicine and
he knew everything about it!

238
00:13:31,286 --> 00:13:32,579
But that's different.

239
00:13:32,621 --> 00:13:37,166
[ALL laughing]

240
00:13:51,431 --> 00:13:54,850
[ALBERT] You want me to select
an orphan to be our ward?

241
00:13:54,892 --> 00:13:56,936
Are you sure?

242
00:13:58,020 --> 00:13:59,897
[EARL MORIARTY]
You heard me.

243
00:13:59,939 --> 00:14:02,275
Since you regularly
volunteer at that school,

244
00:14:02,317 --> 00:14:03,735
you know their stock.

245
00:14:03,777 --> 00:14:06,738
Go and find us
a suitable candidate.

246
00:14:06,780 --> 00:14:08,531
Preferably one
that's well behaved

247
00:14:08,573 --> 00:14:12,243
and won't cause us any trouble.

248
00:14:12,285 --> 00:14:14,954
Yes, Father.

249
00:14:22,420 --> 00:14:23,797
Uh?

250
00:14:23,838 --> 00:14:25,507
[WILLIAM] According
to these calculations,

251
00:14:25,548 --> 00:14:29,260
one person can advance at a rate
of roughly ten inches per hour.

252
00:14:29,302 --> 00:14:31,887
Taking into account
the firmness of the soil,

253
00:14:31,929 --> 00:14:34,474
in order to dig
a distance of 40 yards

254
00:14:34,516 --> 00:14:36,851
it would require
a week of labour.

255
00:14:36,892 --> 00:14:40,396
[ALBERT]
Sounds rather complicated to me.

256
00:14:40,438 --> 00:14:42,440
[WILLIAM]
Lord Moriarty.

257
00:14:42,482 --> 00:14:44,359
[MEN grumble]

258
00:14:44,400 --> 00:14:46,152
[WORKMAN 2D]
What do ya say, fellas?

259
00:14:46,194 --> 00:14:50,072
I guess we'd better get
back to our work then, huh?

260
00:14:50,114 --> 00:14:51,324
[WORKMAN 2B]
If we don't get a move on,

261
00:14:51,366 --> 00:14:54,703
we'll never finish today.

262
00:14:54,744 --> 00:14:56,454
I hope I wasn't interrupting.

263
00:14:56,496 --> 00:15:00,041
No. Anyway, we finished
the most important part.

264
00:15:00,082 --> 00:15:02,251
You're like an advisor
for the townspeople.

265
00:15:02,293 --> 00:15:06,381
Actually, more of a
consultant, I would say.

266
00:15:06,422 --> 00:15:09,258
The truth is this is
how I've gotten by.

267
00:15:09,300 --> 00:15:13,847
When I help others, they give
me food or money in gratitude.

268
00:15:13,888 --> 00:15:18,142
It's a more peaceful way of
living as opposed to stealing.

269
00:15:18,184 --> 00:15:22,104
Anyway, if I come across
someone in a dire situation,

270
00:15:22,146 --> 00:15:23,857
and I'm able to help them,

271
00:15:23,898 --> 00:15:26,860
then I like to do
whatever I can for them.

272
00:15:30,946 --> 00:15:32,365
Did you hear the news?

273
00:15:32,407 --> 00:15:35,535
Someone managed to sneak into
Old Mister Thompson's strongbox.

274
00:15:35,577 --> 00:15:36,994
[MAN 2B]
Yeah, I did.

275
00:15:37,036 --> 00:15:39,831
Apparently, they made off with
all the valuables in his cellar.

276
00:15:39,873 --> 00:15:42,917
So, some thief stole the money
he extorted from the poor.

277
00:15:42,958 --> 00:15:44,168
It serves him right.

278
00:15:44,210 --> 00:15:45,253
Still though,

279
00:15:45,294 --> 00:15:47,380
the bloke who did it
must be something else.

280
00:15:47,422 --> 00:15:49,131
[MAN 2C]
I mean, digging a side tunnel

281
00:15:49,173 --> 00:15:50,592
from the sewer to sneak in.

282
00:15:50,633 --> 00:15:52,218
[MAN 2B]
Got that right.

283
00:15:52,260 --> 00:15:55,805
No small feat,
tunnelling for 40 yards.

284
00:15:55,847 --> 00:15:57,473
[ALBERT gasps]

285
00:16:02,228 --> 00:16:05,440
No small feat,
tunnelling for 40 yards.

286
00:16:07,233 --> 00:16:09,694
[WILLIAM] In order to dig
a distance of forty yards,

287
00:16:09,736 --> 00:16:12,572
it would require
a week of labour.

288
00:16:12,614 --> 00:16:17,118
[ALBERT] Impossible.
It's only my imagination.

289
00:16:17,159 --> 00:16:21,372
Welcome. Lord Moriarty,
how lovely to see you again.

290
00:16:21,414 --> 00:16:23,040
Is something the matter?

291
00:16:23,082 --> 00:16:25,084
Your complexion is
rather pale today.

292
00:16:25,126 --> 00:16:27,211
I-- No, I'm fine.

293
00:16:27,253 --> 00:16:30,465
You can find all the children
assembled in the chapel.

294
00:16:36,679 --> 00:16:38,473
[BOTH]
Hm?

295
00:16:40,433 --> 00:16:43,978
[GIRL 2A] Then, the nobility
are good people, right?

296
00:16:44,019 --> 00:16:47,690
[WILLIAM] Very true. Every day,
they wear the finest clothes,

297
00:16:47,732 --> 00:16:52,027
eat delicious food, and fall
asleep in their warm beds.

298
00:16:52,069 --> 00:16:54,155
That's on account
of their forefathers

299
00:16:54,196 --> 00:16:57,116
and their contributions
to this great land.

300
00:16:57,158 --> 00:16:58,827
The upper-class are important.

301
00:16:58,868 --> 00:17:00,077
[GIRL 2A]
Mm-hm.

302
00:17:00,119 --> 00:17:02,914
Perhaps, but they're
not all good.

303
00:17:02,956 --> 00:17:05,124
They sneer down at me
from their carriages.

304
00:17:05,166 --> 00:17:07,126
Treat me like rubbish.

305
00:17:07,251 --> 00:17:08,503
My poor mother.

306
00:17:08,544 --> 00:17:11,213
She worked for a rich man
and he put her through hell.

307
00:17:11,255 --> 00:17:13,967
She ended up dying
because of it.

308
00:17:14,008 --> 00:17:18,763
You're right. They are human,
and can be corrupted.

309
00:17:19,806 --> 00:17:23,852
[WILLIAM] So then, what happens
when a bad nobleman shows up?

310
00:17:23,893 --> 00:17:26,938
Well? Remember what
I told you before?

311
00:17:26,980 --> 00:17:27,939
We fight 'em!

312
00:17:27,981 --> 00:17:28,857
[ALBERT gasps]

313
00:17:28,898 --> 00:17:30,733
[CHILDREN]
We fight and kill 'em all!

314
00:17:30,775 --> 00:17:32,735
Fight the rich! Kill the rich!

315
00:17:32,777 --> 00:17:35,112
Bring death to
every rotten noble!

316
00:17:35,154 --> 00:17:37,114
Fight the rich! Kill the rich!

317
00:17:37,156 --> 00:17:39,408
Bring death to
every rotten noble!

318
00:17:39,450 --> 00:17:41,452
Fight the rich! Kill the rich!

319
00:17:41,494 --> 00:17:43,245
Bring death to
every rotten noble!

320
00:17:43,287 --> 00:17:46,457
[ALBERT] All we have to do
to make this country perfect

321
00:17:46,499 --> 00:17:49,293
is to rid it of villainy.

322
00:17:49,335 --> 00:17:50,879
Under God's watchful eye

323
00:17:50,920 --> 00:17:54,465
he sat there,
preaching the ways of evil

324
00:17:54,507 --> 00:17:57,510
in order to make the world just.

325
00:18:04,976 --> 00:18:06,352
[ALBERT gasps]

326
00:18:06,394 --> 00:18:08,270
Oh. I'm terribly sorry!

327
00:18:08,312 --> 00:18:09,814
[gasps]

328
00:18:09,856 --> 00:18:11,691
[MAID 2A cries]

329
00:18:12,984 --> 00:18:16,362
[WILLIAM]
You're back home early, Brother.

330
00:18:16,404 --> 00:18:18,072
What have you done?

331
00:18:18,113 --> 00:18:19,448
[WILLIAM]
Nothing of consequence.

332
00:18:19,490 --> 00:18:22,076
Just disciplining
a careless servant.

333
00:18:22,117 --> 00:18:23,828
I swear, these people...

334
00:18:23,870 --> 00:18:26,163
They bring it upon
themselves, you know.

335
00:18:26,205 --> 00:18:28,875
They can't even make a cup
of tea without screwing up.

336
00:18:28,917 --> 00:18:31,252
Suppose I'll have
to talk to Father

337
00:18:31,293 --> 00:18:34,005
and have her put
back on the streets.

338
00:18:34,047 --> 00:18:36,131
[ALBERT growls]

339
00:18:46,976 --> 00:18:49,520
[ALBERT] You're like an
advisor for the townspeople.

340
00:18:49,562 --> 00:18:55,818
Actually, more of a
consultant, I would say.

341
00:18:55,860 --> 00:18:58,988
[WILLIAM] The truth is,
this is how I've gotten by.

342
00:18:59,030 --> 00:19:03,618
When I help others, they give me
food or money in gratitude.

343
00:19:07,080 --> 00:19:08,915
I swear, these people,..

344
00:19:08,957 --> 00:19:11,834
They bring it upon
themselves, you know.

345
00:19:13,753 --> 00:19:14,629
We fight 'em!

346
00:19:14,670 --> 00:19:16,297
[CHILDREN]
We fight and kill 'em all!

347
00:19:16,338 --> 00:19:18,591
Bring death to
every rotten noble!

348
00:19:18,633 --> 00:19:19,884
[ALBERT gasps]

349
00:19:19,926 --> 00:19:22,762
[WILLIAM] If I come across
someone in a dire situation,

350
00:19:22,804 --> 00:19:24,639
and I'm able to help them,

351
00:19:24,680 --> 00:19:27,558
then I like to do
whatever I can for them.

352
00:19:27,600 --> 00:19:29,727
[ALBERT gasps]

353
00:19:40,154 --> 00:19:42,615
So you wanted to speak with me?

354
00:19:44,033 --> 00:19:47,453
Death to every rotten noble.

355
00:19:47,495 --> 00:19:50,247
You overheard that, did you?

356
00:19:50,289 --> 00:19:52,583
I found it to be an
outstanding lecture.

357
00:19:52,625 --> 00:19:55,795
Still, making an enemy
of the nobility

358
00:19:55,837 --> 00:19:59,173
means picking a quarrel
with the whole country.

359
00:19:59,214 --> 00:20:00,967
Can you fight them?

360
00:20:01,009 --> 00:20:02,885
The entire system?

361
00:20:02,927 --> 00:20:04,929
[WILLIAM chuckles]

362
00:20:09,475 --> 00:20:12,103
That's the idea.

363
00:20:13,104 --> 00:20:16,107
[WILLIAM] Our lives should
be seen as equal in value.

364
00:20:16,149 --> 00:20:18,359
We all deserve the
right to happiness.

365
00:20:18,400 --> 00:20:21,612
But in this country,
that possibility doesn't exist.

366
00:20:21,654 --> 00:20:24,406
It's a luxury only
the rich can afford.

367
00:20:24,448 --> 00:20:27,535
This class system is
a curse on civilization.

368
00:20:27,576 --> 00:20:31,080
It causes people's hearts to
become poisoned and twisted.

369
00:20:31,122 --> 00:20:34,583
As a result,
it gives rise to devils.

370
00:20:34,625 --> 00:20:38,420
Therefore, the opposite
must also be true.

371
00:20:38,462 --> 00:20:41,924
Once the demon is exorcised,
the heart can become pure

372
00:20:41,966 --> 00:20:43,759
and the curse, lifted.

373
00:20:43,801 --> 00:20:48,389
Mark my words,
this country will be purified.

374
00:20:49,640 --> 00:20:52,309
[WILLIAM] Are you going to
report me to Scotland Yard?

375
00:20:52,351 --> 00:20:53,895
[ALBERT gasps]

376
00:20:53,936 --> 00:20:57,815
[WILLIAM] As a nobleman,
I bet you'd like that--

377
00:20:57,857 --> 00:21:01,027
to see me taken
away in shackles.

378
00:22:42,670 --> 00:22:43,879
[ALBERT]
An orphan,

379
00:22:43,921 --> 00:22:47,341
with the intellect to leave
even adults speechless.

380
00:22:47,382 --> 00:22:51,637
Initially, I simply regarded
him as some sort of prodigy.

381
00:22:51,679 --> 00:22:53,347
That was all.

382
00:22:53,388 --> 00:22:56,100
Nothing else triggered
my interest.

383
00:22:56,142 --> 00:22:59,770
But that soon changed.

384
00:23:18,455 --> 00:23:20,749
Look! There're survivors!

385
00:23:20,791 --> 00:23:23,669
Doctor! Quickly!

386
00:23:23,711 --> 00:23:25,171
[FIREMAN 2A]
Are you all right?

387
00:23:25,213 --> 00:23:27,381
My name is Albert.

388
00:23:27,422 --> 00:23:31,677
Albert James Moriarty.

389
00:23:31,719 --> 00:23:34,805
And these two...

390
00:23:34,847 --> 00:23:37,391
[ALBERT] Next time
on Moriarty the Patriot  .

391
00:23:37,432 --> 00:23:40,311
"The Scarlet Eyes Act Two."
